{"name":"Cameo Stone","hex":"#ebdfd8","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cameo","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A piece of jewelry, etc., carved in relief.","A single very brief appearance, especially by a prominent celebrity in a movie or song."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To appear in a cameo role."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cameo"},{"term":"stone","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hard earthen substance that can form rocks; especially, such substance when regarded as a building material.","A piece of such material: a block of stone, rock or a pebble."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Constructed of stone.","Having the appearance of stone."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/stone"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cameo (carving)","blocks":[{"text":"Cameo is a method of carving an object such as an engraved gem, item of jewellery or vessel. It nearly always features a raised (positive) relief image; contrast with intaglio, which has a negative image. Originally, and still in discussing historical work, cameo only referred to works where the relief image was of a contrasting colour to the background; this was achieved by carefully carving a piece of material with a flat plane where two contrasting colours met, removing all the first colour except for the image to leave a contrasting background."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Great_Cameo_of_France_CdM_Paris_Bab264_n1.jpg/330px-Great_Cameo_of_France_CdM_Paris_Bab264_n1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cameo_(carving)"},{"title":"Rock (geology)","blocks":[{"text":"In geology, a rock is any naturally occurring solid mass or aggregate of minerals or mineraloid matter. It is categorized by the minerals included, its chemical composition, and the way in which it is formed. Rocks form the Earth's outer solid layer, the crust, and most of its interior, except for the liquid outer core and pockets of magma in the asthenosphere. The study of rocks involves multiple subdisciplines of geology, including petrology and mineralogy. It may be limited to rocks found on Earth, or it may include planetary geology that studies the rocks of other celestial objects."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg/330px-Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rock_(geology)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Adlerkameo_KHM.jpg/500px-Adlerkameo_KHM.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Adlerkameo_KHM.jpg/1280px-Adlerkameo_KHM.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Adlerkameo_KHM.jpg/500px-Adlerkameo_KHM.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Eagle Cameo, Roman 27 B.C. Two-layered onyx.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAdlerkameo_KHM.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Cameo_August_BM_Gem3577.jpg/500px-Cameo_August_BM_Gem3577.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Cameo_August_BM_Gem3577.jpg/1280px-Cameo_August_BM_Gem3577.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Cameo_August_BM_Gem3577.jpg/500px-Cameo_August_BM_Gem3577.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cameo of Roman Emperor Augustus wearing a gorgoneion and a sword-belt. Three-layered sardonyx cam